WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.412
WHERE and HAVING both filter, but they operate at different stages.

00:00:04.412 --> 00:00:08.687
WHERE filters individual source rows before grouping happens.

00:00:08.687 --> 00:00:17.487
For example, keeping only sales where price is at least twenty-five dollars removes rows before the category

00:00:17.487 --> 00:00:21.332
totals are calculated. HAVING filters completed groups after aggregation.

00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:29.572
For example, keeping categories whose total quantity is greater than ten requires the grouped total to exist

00:00:29.572 --> 00:00:29.942
first.

00:00:29.942 --> 00:00:36.022
A simple test is to ask whether the condition refers to an individual row or to a group

00:00:36.022 --> 00:00:39.052
measure. If it is a row condition, think WHERE.

00:00:39.052 --> 00:00:44.482
If it is a condition on COUNT, SUM, AVG, or another grouped measure, think HAVING.

00:00:44.482 --> 00:00:51.022
Keeping these roles separate makes aggregation queries much easier to reason about.

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:57.457
WHERE filters detail rows before aggregation; HAVING filters groups after aggregation.

00:00:57.457 --> 00:01:02.772
A practical way to choose is to ask whether the condition refers to an individual record or to

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:07.147
a summary such as count, average, or total.

00:01:07.147 --> 00:01:10.391
Mixing those levels is a common source of confusion.
